From 828ec755f98711dedd3be184bca54b39e8920c23 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Artyom Shalkhakov <artyom.shalkhakov@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 17:14:08 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Read and write a formatter's settings in a keyed archive as
 macOS does

macOS archives a number or date formatter's settings in an NS.attributes
dictionary keyed by property name (formatterBehavior, numberStyle,
generatesDecimalNumbers, maximumFractionDigits, minimum, locale,
dateStyle, timeStyle, dateFormat and so on), beside 10.0 keys such as
NS.positiveformat, NS.decimal and NS.format.  NSNumberFormatter read only
the 10.0 keys and NSDateFormatter read a keyed archive as an unkeyed one,
so a formatter from a nib came out with the default style and digits: a
decimal field that generates decimal numbers parsed to a double, and a
date field lost its styles.  Neither wrote a keyed archive macOS could
read.

Reading: each property NS.attributes names that the formatter has a
setter for is set by key-value coding, the behaviour, locale and style
first, since a style resets the digits and the format, then the rest in
the order of their names.  With the 10.4 behaviour the 10.0 keys only
repeat those settings, as they stood in the locale the archive was
written in, so they are read for a 10.0 formatter only; NS.decimal no
longer pins "." over a de_DE locale.  NSDateFormatter reads NS.format and
NS.natural for a 10.0 formatter.

Writing: NS.attributes holds what differs from a new formatter with the
same locale and style, as macOS writes what was set, with
formatterBehavior and allowsFloats always, and a BOOL as a boolean; the
10.0 keys are written too.  A 10.0 formatter writes what macOS writes
for one (dateFormat_10_0 for a date formatter).

The keys, values and types are those macOS 15 archives.
Tests/base/NSNumberFormatter/keyedAttributes.m and
Tests/base/NSDateFormatter/keyedAttributes.m read archives of that shape,
and check what is written and read back; every check also passes on
macOS.  Without the change 20 of the 27 number checks and 15 of the 21
date checks fail.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
